  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "The query is a highly ionized amidine/carboxylate zwitterion (logD -7.48, TPSA 148.6, neutral fraction 0), and close L1 analogs with the same amidine/carboxylate motif had low human absolute oral bioavailability: DX-9065a F=2–3% (C04, C14), 2.8±1.5% as a conventional capsule (C06), 3% in a capsule study (C07); peramivir <3% in humans (C03, C22) and IV-delivered due to low F (C31); an amidine/carboxylate compound was not orally viable in humans (C21).",
      "card_ids": ["C04","C06","C07","C14","C03","C22","C31","C21"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L4 permeability records for structurally similar ionized peptidomimetics show low absorptive permeability: inogatran (guanidine/carboxylate/cyclohexyl) absorptive Papp 0.34±0.07×10^-6 cm/s with higher secretory flux (C61); enalaprilat PAMPA flux <0.01×10^-6 cm/s (C60); pentamidine-related AP-BL Papp 1.86±1.19×10^-8 cm/s, only 4.42±0.43×10^-8 with CsA (C51, C56, C57, C59); DB75 AP-BL 3.8±0.2×10^-7 with BL-AP 8.5±0.2×10^-7 cm/s (C53, C58).",
      "card_ids": ["C61","C60","C51","C56","C57","C59","C53","C58"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L5 gut-wall/intestinal records add mechanistic support: human jejunal Peff for inogatran 0.03×10^-4 cm/s (C65) and enalaprilat 0.013±0.009×10^-4 cm/s (C64) are very low; a related compound had Papp A→B <0.7×10^-6 cm/s with efflux ratio 46 (C69); atenolol low-permeability standards range about 0.2–0.6×10^-6 cm/s (C62, C63, C66, C67, C68, C72, C73).",
      "card_ids": ["C65","C64","C69","C62","C63","C66","C67","C68","C72","C73"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "C71 (dabigatran active moiety not a P-gp substrate) and C70 (saquinavir Papp 2.25×10^-6 cm/s) are not query-specific and do not overturn the direct low-F amidine/carboxylate analogs; they only limit a P-gp-specific mechanism and do not provide affirmative evidence for F≥20%.",
      "card_ids": ["C71","C70"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "No direct oral bioavailability measurement exists for the query, and no fed-state-specific data are available; the low prediction rests on transferable direct low-F analogs and consistent permeability/efflux evidence, so confidence remains moderate.",
      "card_ids": ["C04","C03","C21","C61","C64","C65","C69"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "gut_wall_efflux_intestinal_metabolism",
      "applicability": "moderate",
      "direction": "supportive",
      "decision_effect": "strengthened",
      "card_ids": ["C62","C63","C64","C65","C66","C67","C68","C69","C72","C73"]
    },
    {
      "family": "gut_wall_efflux_intestinal_metabolism",
      "applicability": "low",
      "direction": "neutral_or_unclear",
      "decision_effect": "no_change",
      "card_ids": ["C70","C71"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct oral bioavailability measurement exists for the query; the decision relies on structurally similar analogs and physicochemical priors.",
    "No fed-state-specific bioavailability, permeability, or efflux data are available; all relevant records are from no_reported_external_condition.",
    "No query-specific transporter or enzyme identification is available; P-gp status is inferred from analogs, and C71 suggests not all amidine/carboxylate actives are P-gp substrates.",
    "No query-specific formulation or dose data are available; conventional capsule formulation worsened DX-9065a F, but no query formulation was tested.",
    "L4/L5 permeability and efflux records are for reference molecules, not the query, and transferability is inferred from structural similarity."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "Predicted low: direct human low-F amidine/carboxylate analogs (DX-9065a F=2–3%, peramivir <3%, C21 not orally viable) plus L4/L5 permeability and gut-wall efflux evidence for close ionized peptidomimetics affirmatively support F<20%. Lisinopril's 25% absorption and dabigatran's lack of P-gp interaction are limited counterexamples but do not outweigh the direct analogs. Fed-state and query-specific data remain absent, so confidence is moderate."
}

